1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,314
Someone rang the doorbell expecting a cash pickup.
But

2
00:00:03,314 --> 00:00:06,627
the house looked like a funeral. The “dead man”

3
00:00:06,627 --> 00:00:09,940
was fictional. The grieving family were two
YouTubers, waiting

4
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:13,254
with questions. In that Los Angeles sting, the
visitor

5
00:00:13,254 --> 00:00:16,568
was Jiandong Chen, later sentenced to six and a

6
00:00:16,568 --> 00:00:19,881
half years in federal prison. The twist is bigger

7
00:00:19,881 --> 00:00:23,194
than one awkward confrontation: prosecutors say the
cash pickup

8
00:00:23,194 --> 00:00:26,508
was one visible piece of a fraud network that

9
00:00:26,508 --> 00:00:29,822
took more than twenty-seven million dollars from
over two

10
00:00:29,822 --> 00:00:30,926
thousand older Americans.

11
00:00:31,576 --> 00:00:34,890
So how did a refund turn into a box

12
00:00:34,890 --> 00:00:35,626
of cash?

13
00:00:36,276 --> 00:00:39,589
Between 2021 and 2023, prosecutors say,
victims—often in their

14
00:00:39,589 --> 00:00:42,903
seventies and eighties—received unsolicited calls,
emails or computer pop-ups.

15
00:00:42,903 --> 00:00:46,216
The message might claim there was a problem with

16
00:00:46,216 --> 00:00:49,530
tech support, a bank account, a government issue, or

17
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:52,843
a small retail charge. The goal was simple: get

18
00:00:52,843 --> 00:00:56,157
the person to call a number controlled by the

19
00:00:56,157 --> 00:00:56,525
scammers.

20
00:00:56,905 --> 00:01:00,359
Then came the trust-building. Some victims were told
they’d

21
00:01:00,359 --> 00:01:03,812
receive a refund. A screen appeared to show money

22
00:01:03,812 --> 00:01:07,266
sent back by mistake. The caller said: “You were

23
00:01:07,266 --> 00:01:10,720
overpaid. Send the extra back.” But the supposed
refund

24
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:14,174
was never real. The victims were being pushed to

25
00:01:14,174 --> 00:01:17,628
send their own money—by wire or, sometimes, cash in

26
00:01:17,628 --> 00:01:18,395
the mail.

27
00:01:19,045 --> 00:01:22,499
That is where the story becomes a physical delivery

28
00:01:22,499 --> 00:01:25,953
system. According to the Justice Department, victims
were given

29
00:01:25,953 --> 00:01:29,406
fake recipient names and addresses at retail
locations that

30
00:01:29,406 --> 00:01:32,860
accepted express packages. Chen admitted using fake
IDs matching

31
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:36,314
those names to pick up packages of cash. Some

32
00:01:36,314 --> 00:01:39,768
money was collected in person. Prosecutors say the
broader

33
00:01:39,768 --> 00:01:43,221
network also used remote desktop software to access
computers,

34
00:01:43,221 --> 00:01:46,675
and cryptocurrency to move proceeds to overseas
associates and

35
00:01:46,675 --> 00:01:47,443
pay co-conspirators.

36
00:01:47,823 --> 00:01:51,451
The fake-funeral video captured one attempted
pickup. Creators Ashton

37
00:01:51,451 --> 00:01:55,080
Bingham and Art Kulik, from Trilogy Media,
confronted Chen

38
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:58,708
at a home in the Los Angeles area. It

39
00:01:58,708 --> 00:02:02,337
was a dramatic moment—but not the whole case, and

40
00:02:02,337 --> 00:02:05,966
not proof by itself. The Justice Department says
prosecutors

41
00:02:05,966 --> 00:02:09,594
also reviewed videos of Chen collecting packages and
counting

42
00:02:09,594 --> 00:02:13,223
money. Their account traces his involvement from
early 2023

43
00:02:13,223 --> 00:02:16,851
through December of that year, including an
attempted pickup

44
00:02:16,851 --> 00:02:19,674
in Upland after an earlier local arrest.

45
00:02:20,324 --> 00:02:23,952
In April 2026, Chen pleaded guilty. On November 13,

46
00:02:23,952 --> 00:02:27,581
he was sentenced to 78 months—six and a half

47
00:02:27,581 --> 00:02:31,210
years. He admitted that losses during his role in

48
00:02:31,210 --> 00:02:34,838
the conspiracy exceeded nine and a half million
dollars.

49
00:02:34,838 --> 00:02:38,467
Investigators identified more than two thousand
victims and losses

50
00:02:38,467 --> 00:02:41,289
above twenty-seven million across the wider scheme.

51
00:02:41,939 --> 00:02:45,568
That difference matters: the twenty-seven million
describes the wider

52
00:02:45,568 --> 00:02:49,196
operation; the nine-and-a-half-million figure is
tied to Chen’s admitted

53
00:02:49,196 --> 00:02:52,825
period of involvement. The judge said the harm was

54
00:02:52,825 --> 00:02:56,454
more than money—victims also lost trust, dignity and
peace

55
00:02:56,454 --> 00:02:57,260
of mind.

56
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:00,870
The lesson is not “trust no one.” It’s: pause

57
00:03:00,870 --> 00:03:04,099
when someone creates urgency around money. If a
company

58
00:03:04,099 --> 00:03:07,329
says you’ve been over-refunded, hang up and contact
it

59
00:03:07,329 --> 00:03:10,559
using a number from its official website or your

60
00:03:10,559 --> 00:03:13,788
card. Never mail cash to fix a computer or

61
00:03:13,788 --> 00:03:17,018
return a surprise refund. Don’t give a caller remote

62
00:03:17,018 --> 00:03:20,248
access to your device. And tell someone you trust

63
00:03:20,248 --> 00:03:22,042
before moving a large amount.

64
00:03:22,692 --> 00:03:25,921
A viral sting can make one face recognizable. But

65
00:03:25,921 --> 00:03:29,151
the evidence—victim accounts, records, investigators
and court proceedings—shows the

66
00:03:29,151 --> 00:03:32,381
system behind it. The most important question isn’t
how

67
00:03:32,381 --> 00:03:35,610
clever the scam sounded. It’s whether the target
gets

68
00:03:35,610 --> 00:03:38,840
a chance to stop, verify, and ask for help.

